    "raw_model_answer": "Both the Leyden jar and Franklin bells are historical devices related to the study of static electricity, but they serve distinct functions based on their operating principles.\n\n**Leyden Jar**\n*   **Operating Principle:** The Leyden jar functions as the **original form of a capacitor** (originally known as a \"condenser\"). It stores static electricity between two electrodes: one metal foil cemented to the inside surface of a glass jar and another on the outside surface. A metal terminal projects through the lid to make contact with the inner foil, completing the circuit.\n*   **Use:** It is a storage device designed to hold an electric charge.\n\n**Franklin Bell (Gordon's Bells / Lightning Bell)**\n*   **Operating Principle:** Franklin bells are designed to **convert electrical energy into mechanical energy**. When charged by a Leyden jar, they cause the clapper of a bell to oscillate back and forth between two oppositely charged bells. Unlike a Leyden jar, they do not store energy but rather demonstrate the effects of charge through motion.\n*   **Use:** They serve as a **qualitative indicator of electric charge** and were used for simple demonstrations of continuous mechanical motion rather than for scientific research.\n\nIn summary, the Leyden jar is a storage device that accumulates charge, while the Franklin bell is a demonstration device that converts that charge into mechanical motion.\n\n<ref id=\"c1\"/>\n<ref id=\"c2\"/>",
    "persisted_final_answer": "Both the Leyden jar and Franklin bells are historical devices related to the study of static electricity, but they serve distinct functions based on their operating principles.\n\n**Leyden Jar**\n*   **Operating Principle:** The Leyden jar functions as the **original form of a capacitor** (originally known as a \"condenser\"). It stores static electricity between two electrodes: one metal foil cemented to the inside surface of a glass jar and another on the outside surface. A metal terminal projects through the lid to make contact with the inner foil, completing the circuit.\n*   **Use:** It is a storage device designed to hold an electric charge.\n\n**Franklin Bell (Gordon's Bells / Lightning Bell)**\n*   **Operating Principle:** Franklin bells are designed to **convert electrical energy into mechanical energy**. When charged by a Leyden jar, they cause the clapper of a bell to oscillate back and forth between two oppositely charged bells. Unlike a Leyden jar, they do not store energy but rather demonstrate the effects of charge through motion.\n*   **Use:** They serve as a **qualitative indicator of electric charge** and were used for simple demonstrations of continuous mechanical motion rather than for scientific research.\n\nIn summary, the Leyden jar is a storage device that accumulates charge, while the Franklin bell is a demonstration device that converts that charge into mechanical motion.\n\n<kb doc=\"1c5fa1db320d59cba389daa592d258d7cd237eca31134a4f70efd152d7d06f4f\" chunk_id=\"e8a751ff-032c-4c8c-bb31-4eddc73b3a86\" kb_id=\"96a9eebd-ffb2-4bc6-9bea-3ed015845479\" />\n<kb doc=\"0f94a2d0a92bd4a9f9e3672bba1af95e35b3f0ff0de86ca8c80a71d78f38936f\" chunk_id=\"0f650d87-44cc-4fe5-8a91-30844d2578e0\" kb_id=\"96a9eebd-ffb2-4bc6-9bea-3ed015845479\" />"
